---
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import TargetGrid from "../components/TargetGrid.astro";

// The platform guide: the fourth of the four ways. A platform is the host seam —
// chosen when the application is built, never swapped at runtime — and it is the
// one place where "bring your own" means writing a `Platform` impl rather than
// implementing an algorithm.
//
// The built-ins below are the four the facade compiles in, plus the two test
// harnesses. The mobile path is the third-party `gpui-mobile` crate; it is not
// ported to these crates yet, so it is named without a link and marked as such.

const stats = [
  {
    value: "4",
    label: "Built-in platforms",
    note: "macOS, Windows, Linux, Web",
    win: true,
  },
  {
    value: "1",
    label: "Host seam",
    note: "Platform, in gpui_platform",
  },
  {
    value: "0",
    label: "Drivers linked",
    note: "The leaf links nothing",
  },
  {
    value: "1",
    label: "Hook",
    note: "Application::with_platform",
  },
];

const harnesses = [
  {
    name: "TestPlatform",
    where: "gpui_authoring · test-support",
    does: "The in-process platform the test harness draws through: deterministic dispatchers, a simulated display and window, and no display server.",
  },
  {
    name: "Headless Linux",
    where: "gpui_linux · ZED_HEADLESS",
    does: "A platform with no compositor surface: layout, text shaping and entity plumbing run normally, and draw discards the scene.",
  },
];

const owns = [
  "the event loop, and when a frame is asked for",
  "window creation, its raw handles, and the swapchain",
  "displays, their bounds and their refresh intervals",
  "the clipboard, drag and drop, and the input queue",
  "the GPU backend's devices, and the renderer a window draws through",
  "screen capture, thermal state and system appearance",
];
---

<Page
  title="Bring your own platform — bite-gpui"
  description="Run the same stack on Metal, Direct3D, wgpu, X11/Wayland or wasm — or implement Platform and bring the host yourself. The platform is the one seam an application chooses at build time."
  kicker="Ways to extend · Serve"
  heading="Use one of ours,"
  accent="or bring your own."
  lede="The platform is the host seam: it supplies the event loop, the windows, the displays, the clipboard and the GPU backend the scene draws through. Four ship and two harnesses exist for tests; a platform of your own is a Platform impl, not a fork."
  current="serve"
  stats={stats}
>
  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="kicker">Built in</span>
      <h2>One backend per build, selected by cfg.</h2>
      <p>
        A platform is never chosen by a manifest line or a runtime string. The
        facade's platform bundle enables the one for the target, and the
        application is constructed with it — which is why the same source
        compiles on all four. The two mobile slabs are the exception: they are
        a third-party platform crate, not a backend in this tree.
      </p>
    </div>

    <TargetGrid />

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The wgpu renderer is the one two platforms share: Linux always draws
        through it, and the web reaches WebGPU through the same crate. Metal and
        Direct3D are native backends with their own command encoders.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="kicker">What a platform owns</span>
      <h2>Everything a plain window cannot name.</h2>
      <p>
        The trait is the contract between the facade and a backend. It is
        declared in <span class="mono">gpui_platform</span>, the acyclic leaf
        that links no drivers — a backend implements it without depending on the
        framework.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <ul class="checklist">
        {owns.map((item) => (
          <li>{item}</li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="callout-title">The seam, in one line</span>
      <p>
        <span class="mono">Application::with_platform(Rc&lt;dyn Platform&gt;)</span>
        — the constructor, not a setter. A platform is chosen with the
        application rather than chained onto it. The default is the host's own,
        compiled in by <span class="mono">cfg</span>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="kicker">Bring your own</span>
      <h2>The test harness is the proof it is a seam.</h2>
      <p>
        Two implementations in the tree are not desktop platforms at all, and
        they are what a new backend can be modelled on: neither has a window
        server, and one has no GPU.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="card-grid two">
      {harnesses.map((row) => (
        <article class="card">
          <div class="card-head">
            <h3 class="card-title mono">{row.name}</h3>
            <span class="card-badge">in tree</span>
          </div>
          <p>{row.does}</p>
          <p class="card-note mono">{row.where}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        An out-of-tree platform implements the same trait, gets a
        <span class="mono">WindowId</span> space and a renderer, and is installed
        with <span class="mono">with_platform</span>. The mobile path is the
        third-party <span class="mono">gpui-mobile</span> crate, which targets
        iOS (Metal) and Android (wgpu/Vulkan) — it is not ported to these crates
        yet.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>
          <strong>iOS and Android are not built in.</strong>{" "}
          <span class="mono">gpui_apple</span> already compiles for iOS and
          shares its Metal and text code, but there is no iOS
          <span class="mono">Platform</span> or entrypoint here; Android has no
          backend in this tree.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A platform is a large impl.</strong> The trait covers the
          event loop, window creation, displays, input, clipboard, GPU specs and
          screen capture. It is the widest seam in the stack, and the one where
          a fork is most tempting — which is why it is documented as its own
          way.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The renderer follows the platform.</strong> A backend picks
          the <span class="mono">SceneRenderer</span> its windows draw through;
          that is why a renderer is not a swap today, and why a second one needs
          a door first (<a href="/swaps#open-seams">the open seams</a>).
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="section-head">
      <span class="kicker">Limits</span>
      <h2>What the plate does not do.</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>
          <strong>It links nothing.</strong> The leaf depends only on the
          vocabulary crates, never on a driver or a windowing library. A backend
          does the linking, in its own crate.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It is not a swap at runtime.</strong> There is no
          <span class="mono">request_windowing</span>-style switch between
          backends; a platform is fixed when the application is built. The one
          thing that changes at runtime is windowing mode, not the backend.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>It is not the distribution story.</strong> Versions, crates
          and provenance are the <a href="/compare">comparison</a> and the
          <a href="/#quickstart">quickstart</a>; this page is about the host.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <span class="callout-title">The rest of the ways</span>
      <p>
        <a href="/swaps">Ways to extend</a> has all four, and
        <a href="/shove">Bring your own surface</a> is the pixels side. If what
        you want to change is the algorithm a seam runs, that is a
        <a href="/swaps#swap">swap</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
